    "teaserText": "Armour and sidestepping",
    "startText": "One morning, the quartermaster issued him a gladius and a suit of armour.\n\n[admiration]Anthony admired the sword. [disappointed]It looked a little short, but sat nicely in his hand. Then again, Anthony had large hands, [scoffs] or so he liked to believe.\n\nThe armour was less comfortable. It was also considerably smellier than he had expected, though the quartermaster assured him this was [sarcastic] perfectly normal after what happened to the previous owner.\n\nHis instructor explained the essentials. \n[angry drunk]\"The gladius is for stabbing the enemy, and the armour for keeping one’s organs where nature had put them.\"",
    "winText": "[trusting] As luck would have it, a dog was sniffing around the camp, looking for a bit of excitement. Anthony took to him immediately. [amazed]Finally, someone who was genuinely pleased to see him.[pause][bark]",
    "loseText": "The sword, javelin and armour each have their purpose. Use the javelin at a distance, the gladius up close, and the armour to keep yourself in one piece. It is a simple system, which is why the army has spent centuries trying to teach it.",
